    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Rainforest.</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of simple sentences. Reading: Follow written instructions with visual aids. Phonemic Awareness: Recognize all the letters of the alphabet in isolation and the correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of simple sentences by identifying key details (e.g., "The sloth is slow."). Can follow written instructions by matching written descriptions with the corresponding pictures (e.g., a description of the rainforest and its picture). Can identify the initial and final phonemes of spoken words related to nature and make the correspondence between letters and their sounds at the beginning of words with visual support (e.g., J /dʒ/ "jungle" supported with a picture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large flashcards of rainforest animals and elements (sloth, toucan, monkey, rainforest, river, leaf) from Lesson 1. The teacher says, "Look! What is this?" and points to each card. Students respond chorally or individually with the vocabulary words. The teacher plays a short "Rainforest Animal Sounds" audio clip and asks, "What do you hear?" to activate prior knowledge about rainforest sounds and animals, connecting to the theme "It's the Rainforest."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a sloth and a sentence strip that reads: "The sloth is slow." The teacher points to the words as they read the sentence slowly and clearly, emphasizing the /s/ sound in "sloth." The teacher models by tracing the letter 'S' in the air and connecting it to the word "sloth." The teacher then points to a picture of the rainforest and a sentence strip that reads: "It's the rainforest." The teacher models the pronunciation of /r/ in "rainforest" and traces 'R' in the air. The teacher uses simple actions to convey meaning (e.g., slow hand movement for "slow," large arm gesture for "rainforest").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing picture of sloth) "Is the sloth fast or slow?" (Expected answer: "Slow.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a rainforest picture) "Is this a jungle or a house?" (Expected answer: "Jungle" or "Rainforest.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the letter 'J' card) "What sound does 'J' make in 'jungle'?" (Expected answer: "/dʒ/ sound with visual support).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful poster titled "Welcome to the Rainforest!" The poster features a main image of a rainforest with various animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, frog, snake) and elements (river, leaf, vines) subtly integrated. Simple sentences are written next to each element or animal. For example:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is the rainforest."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A sloth lives here. The sloth is slow."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! A colorful toucan flies."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A green frog is quiet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this? It's a tall tree."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large illustrated poster with short, descriptive sentences. Genre: Informative captions. Register: Simple, declarative. Format: Picture-text association.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension of short, simple descriptive texts and identifying key details with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What place is this?" (pointing to the rainforest). "What animals do you see?" (Students point and say names).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher reads a sentence, e.g., "The sloth is slow." Students point to the sloth in the poster. Then, the teacher asks, "Who is slow?" and students read the word "sloth" on the poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual):</b> The teacher says, "A toucan is blue." (pointing to a colorful toucan). Students show thumbs up for true or thumbs down for false, based on the picture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple sentences, identifying key details, matching words to pictures, recognizing initial phonemes. Grammar: Adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, colorful toucan). Vocabulary: rainforest, sloth, toucan, monkey, frog, snake, leaf, river, live, grow, climb, swing, hide, sleep. Pronunciation: Initial sounds /s/ in sloth, /r/ in rainforest, J /dʒ/ in jungle.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Read and Match:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with two columns. Column 1 has simple sentences (e.g., "The monkey swings on vines." "The frog is green and quiet."). Column 2 has corresponding pictures of the animals performing the actions or showing their traits. Students draw a line to match each sentence to the correct picture. The teacher circulates, assisting students with reading and checking for accuracy. Emphasis on initial sounds like 's' in 'swing' and 'snake'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Completion with Visuals:</b> Students receive another worksheet with sentences that have a blank space and a choice of two words below, along with a supporting picture. Example: "The ____ is slow." (sloth / frog) [Picture of a sloth]. Students read the sentence and the options, then circle the correct word. This reinforces adjectives + nouns and reading comprehension. Example: "This is a tall ____." (leaf / branch) [Picture of a tall branch].</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness: Initial Sound Identification:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard with the letter 'J' and a picture of a "jungle." The teacher says "J-j-jungle." Students repeat. Then, the teacher distributes a worksheet with various pictures (e.g., jaguar, leaf, jungle, jumpy monkey). Below each picture is a blank line. Students read the word or say the word name and then write the initial letter for the sound they hear (e.g., 'J' for jaguar, 'L' for leaf). They also circle the letter that makes the /dʒ/ sound for 'jungle' with teacher guidance.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "Rainforest Explorer's Guide" - Read and Follow Instructions.</b> Students work individually on a "Rainforest Explorer's Guide" activity sheet. This task requires them to read simple instructions and descriptive sentences and then act upon them by coloring, circling, or drawing on a provided rainforest scene or outline. The task connects directly to the Theme: "It's the Rainforest." and Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders" by simulating an exploration. Students will use target vocabulary (e.g., rainforest, sloth, toucan, frog, river, leaf, vines, tall, green, blue) and grammar (adjectives + nouns, present simple).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual "Read and Draw/Color" worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Instructions:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a worksheet with a simple outline drawing of a rainforest scene (trees, a river, a blank space for animals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The worksheet contains 3-4 simple written instructions/descriptions, for example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Color the river blue."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a green leaf on a tall tree."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a slow sloth. Draw a sloth."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The toucan is colorful. Circle the toucan picture." (if a small toucan picture is provided to circle).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each instruction and perform the action. The teacher walks around, observing student work and providing support as needed, encouraging the use of respectful language for nature ("The quiet frog hides.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Student Self-Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a quick "Rainforest Reading Check" exit ticket. This ticket has 2-3 short sentences about rainforest animals/elements and pictures. Students read each sentence and circle the correct picture or draw a simple checkmark next to the matching image. Example: "A monkey swings on the vines." (Picture of monkey swinging / Picture of frog jumping). They also complete a phonemic awareness check by circling the letter that matches the initial sound of a pictured word (e.g., picture of 'jaguar', students circle 'J').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Student correctly matches sentences to pictures (identifying key details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Student follows written instructions (e.g., "Color the river blue," "Draw a leaf").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Student correctly identifies initial letter sounds for words like "jungle" or "jaguar."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Recognition of vocabulary words related to the rainforest.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer with a simple 'Yes' or 'No' or by drawing a happy/sad face next to the prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about the slow sloth."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the letter 'J' in 'jungle'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>(Connecting learning and planning next steps)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you like to read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to read about the rainforest animals?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What worked?</b> The visual aids and simple, repetitive sentence structures significantly helped students with reading comprehension. The read-and-match activities were engaging. The phonemic awareness activity for 'J' was effective with direct modeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson?</b> Some students struggled with distinguishing between similar-looking words. For the next lesson, provide more explicit pre-teaching of tricky words or focus on sight words. Integrate more opportunities for oral repetition of words and sentences before expecting independent reading. Ensure group work allows for peer support in reading.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This reading lesson provided a foundation for understanding simple descriptions of rainforest animals and their actions. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build upon this by having students actively speak about these animals, describing them and expressing preferences using the vocabulary and grammatical structures (adjectives + nouns, present simple) practiced today in a more interactive, communicative setting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>